iew-1"><span
            class="header-section-number">5.2.1</span> Overview</h3>
            <p>The mandatory LIDO elements are sufficient to identify an
            object or work unambiguously, given that metadata elements
            are applied correctly. In most cases, however, providing the
            bare minimum of metadata will usually not be enough to
            enable good retrieval results in terms of findability and
            object discovery. Compared to LIDO, the CDWA standard
            defines some more elements as required, marked “core” in the
            <a
            href="https://www.getty.edu/research/publications/electronic_publications/cdwa/categories.html">CDWA
            Overview of Categories</a>. These are, besides information
            on the creator and creation, particularly metadata for
            classification and subject matter.</p>
            <p><strong>Overview of mandatory and recommended elements in
            LIDO</strong></p>
            <p>Mandatory elements are</p>
            <ul>
            <li><a
            href="https://lido-schema.org/schema/latest/lido.html#lidoRecID">LIDO
            Metadata Record Identifier</a></li>
            <li><a
            href="http://lido-schema.org/schema/latest/lido.html#objectWorkType">Object/Work
            Type</a></li>
            <li><a
            href="http://lido-schema.org/schema/latest/lido.html#titleSet">Title</a></li>
            <li><a
            href="http://lido-schema.org/schema/latest/lido.html#recordID">Record
            ID</a></li>
            <li><a
            href="http://lido-schema.org/schema/latest/lido.html#recordType">Record
            Type</a></li>
            <li><a
            href="http://lido-schema.org/schema/latest/lido.html#recordSource">Record
            Source</a></li>
            </ul>
            <p>It is strongly recommended to provide indexing terms for
            the following elements. These elements are further described
            below.</p>
            <ul>
            <li><a
            href="http://lido-schema.org/schema/latest/lido.html#classification">Classification</a></li>
            <li><a
            href="http://lido-schema.org/schema/latest/lido.html#measurementsSet">Measurements</a></li>
            <li><a
            href="http://lido-schema.org/schema/latest/lido.html#materialsTech">Materials/Techniques</a></li>
            <li><a
            href="http://lido-schema.org/schema/latest/lido.html#event">Event</a></li>
            <li><a
            href="http://lido-schema.org/schema/latest/lido.html#subject">Subject</a></li>
            <li><a
            href="http://lido-schema.org/schema/latest/lido.html#relatedWork">Related
            Works</a></li>
            </ul>
            <p>It is strongly recommended to provide information for <a
            href="http://lido-schema.org/schema/latest/lido.html#rightsWorkSet">Rights
            for Work</a>, <a
            href="http://lido-schema.org/schema/latest/lido.html#recordRights">Rights
            for Record</a>, and <a
            href="http://lido-schema.org/schema/latest/lido.html#rightsResource">Rights
            Resource</a>. For more information see section <a
            href="##administrative-metadata-1"><em>4.4.2 Administrative
            Metadata</em></a>“.</p>
            <p>It is also recommended to provide information for the
            following elements:</p>
            <ul>
            <li><a
            href="http://lido-schema.org/schema/latest/lido.html#inscriptions">Inscriptions</a></li>
            <li><a
            href="http://lido-schema.org/schema/latest/lido.html#repositorySet">Repository</a></li>
            <li><a
            href="http://lido-schema.org/schema/latest/lido.html#objectDescriptionSet">Description</a></li>
            <li><a
            href="http://lido-schema.org/schema/latest/lido.html#recordInfoSet">Record
            Metadata</a></li>
            <li><a
            href="http://lido-schema.org/schema/latest/lido.html#resourceRepresentation">Resource
            Representation</a></li>
            </ul>
            <p>In addition to the mandatory and recommended elements
            above, data producers are encouraged to utilize further
            schema elements in the LIDO record based on the following
            considerations:</p>
            <ul>
            <li>the nature of the items being described;</li>
            <li>the availability of data in the organization’s
            collections management system;</li>
            <li>the requirements of the target portal.</li>
            </ul>
            <h3 data-number="5.2.2" id="classification"><span
            class="header-section-number">5.2.2</span>
            Classification</h3>
            <p><strong>Element name:</strong> &lt;<a
            href="https://lido-schema.org/schema/latest/lido.html#classification">lido:classification</a>&gt;</p>
            <p><strong>Note:</strong> Classification assigns an object
            to one or more classes from a shared class scheme. Like
            &lt;<a
            href="http://lido-schema.org/schema/latest/lido.html#objectWorkType">lido:objectWorkType</a>&gt;,
            the Classification element is used for grouping similar
            objects so that they can be retrieved in a single search
            operation. Unlike Object/WorkType which classifies the
            object at the most specific level suitable, the
            classification element aggregates objects on the basis of
            broad categories. For example, a work of type “oil painting”
            can be classified under “paintings”, a “bas-relief” would be
            assigned to a class “sculptures”, and a “cathedral” should
            be grouped into a category “architecture”.</p>
            <p>Classes can be used to implement browsing facilities in
            digital environments. Examples are the <a
            href="https://www.europeana.eu/en/themes">Europeana
            Themes</a> or the access to the Louvre collections entitled
            <a href="https://collections.louvre.fr/en/">Explore the
            collections</a>. Such categories may also give a first
            impression of what can be expected to be found in the
            database and thus serve as a useful starting point for
            discovery in a Web portal.</p>
            <p>An object can be assigned several classification terms
            from different classification schemes, depending on the
            aspect or point of view. The schemes may vary in the
            division criteria used to arrange the classes, such as by
            period, location, or status of property. An example is the
            categorization of the <em>Mona Lisa</em> in Wikipedia under
            <a
            href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Category:16th-century_portraits">16th-century
            portraits</a>, <a
            href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Category:Portrait_paintings_in_the_Louvre">Portrait
            paintings in the Louvre</a>, and <a
            href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Category:Stolen_works_of_art">Stolen
            works of art</a>.</p>
            <p>Classification categories can refer to different aspects
            under which the object is viewed. Two common aspects are the
            <em>genre or form</em> represented by the object, reflecting
            its <em>isness</em> at a broad level, and the <em>thematic
            context</em> the object is related to, such as a “subject
            category” or a domain-specific grouping. For example, a
            farmhouse like the <a
            href="https://d-nb.info/gnd/7843738-6">Olson House</a> could
            be classified as a “building” by object genre and assigned
            to “agriculture” as subject category; for a religious
            building like the <a
            href="https://d-nb.info/gnd/1173755292">Holy Name of 